Introduction (~5 min)

- Two years ago, I moved positions and began teaching Python instead of R
- As a longtime R and RStudio user, I was a bit terrified of the transition
- Fortunately, that summer Positon entered open beta (thanks to Julia and team)
- I wrote in a blog post that for data science Positron was "just right"
- "VS Code is too general, RStudio is too specific, but Positron is just right"
- But after actually teaching Python, I discovered I was actually in a different story
- A story I'm calling "Positron and the Three Bears"

Bear No. 1: Installing Python (~3 min)

- Make the pain of installing clear, why uv is the best lightweight option
- How I Stopped Worrying and Learned to Love the CLI
- No longer needed! uv bootstrap options in Python

Bear No. 2: Creating GitHub-Friendly Workflows (~3 min)

- Using GitHub for collaboration, creating portfolios
- Make the pain of Jupyter notebooks with GitHub clear
- Quarto not only GitHub-friendly, but also a means to an end (even if the end is a Jupyter Notebook)
- We used to talk about "death by PowerPoint" but now its "death by notebooks"
- Positron's notebook editor for Jupyter Notebooks and Quarto with in-line support (`quarto.inlineOutput.enabled`)

Bear No. 3: Scaling Compute (~3 min)

- Alternative to local installation, while still needing to learn installation and GitHub
- Make the pain of scalable compute clear (personal, clear; vulnerable with the OWL meme)
- Now available on [JupyterHub](https://positron.posit.co/blog/posts/2026-04-03-positron-server-jupyterhub/)
- See [Outgrowing Your Laptop With Positron](https://github.com/austin3dickey/ssh-demo) slides and demo
- Screenshot/GIF showing SSH tunneling

Provide a QR code to the repo (include a `data-stack` link) and thank Joseph
